{:en
 {:title "What physical costs does Kotoba address?"
  :description "IPLD identity, local execution layout, and the costs of moving, storing, verifying and reusing code and values."
  :intro "Kotoba's design emphasizes immutable code, values and state that can be shared and verified across storage, networks and execution hosts. Its physical concerns include movement, persistence, reuse and verification as well as arithmetic. This is a design emphasis, not a claim of optimal performance on a particular device."
  :notice "Design note reviewed on 23 October 2026. Contract and source observations are separate from runtime qualification and performance measurements. English documentation is the source of record; a Japanese version is provided."
  :sections
  [{:title "Across places and execution times"
    :paragraphs ["Comparisons such as CUDA/GPU, Verilog/circuits, C/CPU and SQL/declarative queries mix hardware and computation models. Kotoba separates meaning, identity and authority from its selected execution target."
                 "An object can outlive a process or be stored on a different machine. Content identity gives those uses a common reference, while local representations serve actual execution. This is useful on one machine too; a CID alone does not retrieve content, guarantee availability or grant permission."]}
   {:title "IPLD does not dictate memory layout"
    :paragraphs ["IPLD describes logical values and content-addressed links; DAG-CBOR encodes them as bytes. Neither fixes CPU pointers or GPU buffers. Kotoba distinguishes the checked definition or persisted value from temporary compiler structures."
                 "At the inspected revision, kotoba.form is a typed AST record containing a tag, scalar payloads, children, a source span and raw data. These AST nodes are not all separately stored CID blocks. Definition identity is derived after checking, with the relevant interface, effects and dependencies."]}
   {:title "Identity, location and authority"
    :rows [["ValueCID" "Portable immutable identity of a value."]
           ["Handle" "A bounded reference within this execution, independent of portable identity."]
           ["Capability" "Authority for a concrete operation; possession of a CID or handle does not create it."]]
    :paragraphs ["The runtime contract resolves or interns content into a local representation. Ordinary projections need not hash or fetch a block each time. Calls inside a closed checked native domain can be ordinary typed calls; actual wire, persistence, provider and authority boundaries retain their checks."]}
   {:title "Block size and cache conditions matter"
    :paragraphs ["One IPLD block can contain multiple nodes. Smaller reusable blocks can improve selective access while adding links, hashes and lookups; larger blocks can reduce overhead while transferring unrelated content. Access patterns and measured costs determine the useful granularity."
                 "A pure typed-eval result is keyed by AdmissionCID and input ValueCIDs. Reusing an artifact also depends on its compiler, target and ABI contracts. An earlier result must never substitute for performing an external effect."]}
   {:title "Numeric work uses suitable buffers"
    :paragraphs ["Dense columns and tensors can use contiguous SIMD or GPU buffers without traversing a content-addressed graph in the inner loop. Logical identity, descriptors and ownership remain explicit at the relevant boundary."
                 "Kotoba records partial Arrow, Wasm SIMD and GPU buffer paths, with copies at named ownership boundaries. Those kernel-and-host observations do not establish universal device support, automatic parallelization or zero-copy behavior."]}
   {:title "Measure the whole cost"
    :paragraphs ["Alongside kernel throughput, measure total latency, transferred and copied bytes, blocks fetched and reused, encoding and verification work, and recomputation avoided. Record workload, cache state, target, host, ownership transfers and policy. Include extra verification and layout work when evaluating a reduction in transfer cost."]}]
